{"text": "Noah learns most successfully through digital resources compatible with eye gaze; reduced copying and keyboard demand; chunked tasks with planned breaks; and verbal rehearsal before producing an answer. These strategies make instruction clearer and reduce the amount of energy spent simply gaining access.\n\nThe profile also identifies predictable barriers. Noah may struggle with motor fatigue and pain; rapid-fire questioning; seizure recovery and missed instruction; and inaccessible field trips or classroom layouts. We should not treat these points as a list of personal deficits. Each one directs the team toward a response. For example, a barrier caused by pace may require additional processing time; an environmental barrier may require a quieter, safer, or more accessible setting; and task complexity may require modeling, chunking, direct instruction, or an alternate response.\n\nThe goal is not to remove every productive challenge. The goal is to remove barriers that prevent the student from engaging with the intended learning. Presenter 3 will now connect the profile’s sensory, medical, behavioural, and safety information to the EA’s day-to-day responsibilities.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-D-04.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Noah", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.4375, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "learns", "start": 0.45, "end": 0.85, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 11}, {"text": "most", "start": 0.8625, "end": 1.1125, "start_char": 12, "end_char": 16}, {"text": "successfully", "start": 1.125, "end": 1.8, "start_char": 17, "end_char": 29}, {"text": "through", "start": 1.8125, "end": 2.05, "start_char": 30, "end_char": 37}, {"text": "digital", "start": 2.0625, "end": 2.5, "start_char": 38, "end_char": 45}, {"text": "resources", "start": 2.5125, "end": 3.0875, "start_char": 46, "end_char": 55}, {"text": "compatible", "start": 3.1, "end": 3.7125, "start_char": 56, "end_char": 66}, {"text": "with", "start": 3.725, "end": 3.875